Description: CVE-2026-63387 - Fix out-of-bounds write in dnsname_to_labels
 dnsname_to_labels() in evdns.c could write one byte past the end of the
 destination buffer when writing the terminating zero label. When a DNS
 response reaches exactly 2^16+1 bytes and ends with a name, the offset j
 could equal buf_len, and "buf[j++] = 0" then performed a one-byte
 out-of-bounds (stack) write. This adds a bounds check that returns -2
 (error) when j has reached buf_len before writing the terminating zero.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/libevent/libevent/commit/377b9022c3ac61aa4540b5dc4b70c60bf74c663d
Author: libevent developers
Bug: GHSA-58rx-7448-jw47
Last-Update: 2026-08-23
---
--- a/evdns.c
+++ b/evdns.c
@@ -1671,7 +1671,10 @@ dnsname_to_labels(u8 *const buf, size_t buf_len, off_t j,
 	/* the labels must be terminated by a 0. */
 	/* It's possible that the name ended in a . */
 	/* in which case the zero is already there */
-	if (!j || buf[j-1]) buf[j++] = 0;
+	if ((size_t)j >= buf_len)
+		return -2;
+	if (!j || buf[j-1])
+		buf[j++] = 0;
 	return j;
  overflow:
 	return (-2);
Description: CVE-2026-63383 - Fix out-of-bounds read in decode_tag_internal
 The evrpc tag decoder pulled up at most sizeof(number)+1 bytes from the
 evbuffer but then iterated the decode loop up to the untrusted declared
 length "len". When "len" exceeded the number of bytes actually pulled up,
 decode_tag_internal() read past the end of the pulled-up buffer, allowing
 an attacker to cause an evrpc client or server to read out of bounds when
 decoding a tag. Cap the read loop at the pulled-up buffer length
 (pullup_len) instead of the declared length. Fixes GHSA-fj29-64w6-73h6.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/libevent/libevent/commit/91ed8745eebabdd27592a83d350338a8c4626321
Author: libevent maintainers
